I have a little weather logger on an STM32L072 that I built on weekends, and it works until the battery gets low or the radio is enabled. I want a refactor, but it must remain boring: same packet bytes, same wake/sleep cadence, no heap allocation after boot, and no new RTOS dependency. The current code mixes the interrupt callback, ring-buffer bookkeeping, CRC calculation, and flash-page rotation in one class. Please split the responsibilities into small testable units and explain any ordering or memory-barrier assumptions you rely on.

`firmware/src/TelemetryBuffer.cpp` currently contains:

```cpp
static volatile uint8_t head;
static volatile uint8_t tail;
static Sample slots[16];

void TelemetryBuffer::pushFromIsr(const Sample& s) {
    slots[head] = s;
    head = (head + 1) & 0x0f;
    if (head == tail) {
        tail = (tail + 1) & 0x0f;
        dropped++;
    }
}

bool TelemetryBuffer::pop(Sample& out) {
    __disable_irq();
    if (tail == head) {
        __enable_irq();
        return false;
    }
    out = slots[tail];
    tail = (tail + 1) & 0x0f;
    __enable_irq();
    return true;
}
```

`pushFromIsr()` is called by `drivers/adc_dma.cpp` at 8 kHz. The main loop drains it every 250 ms, then `FlashJournal::append()` writes a 32-byte record. The board has a single-core Cortex-M0+, and `Sample` is currently 12 bytes. There is a watchdog reset if the drain takes longer than 40 ms. The existing tests in `test/telemetry_buffer_test.cpp` run only on the host and do not model an interrupt arriving while `pop()` copies a slot.

The field symptom is rare but real:

```text
[radio=on] buffer overflow=0
[flash] seq=1841 crc=ok
[watchdog] reset reason=IWDG
[boot] replayed seq=1838, expected=1842
[radio=off] no reproduction after 6h
```

Please preserve the public methods used by `app/main.cpp` and the on-flash record format. Decide whether `volatile` is sufficient here, whether the producer can overwrite a partially copied `Sample`, and how a full buffer should be represented without corrupting sequence numbers. Add host tests for wraparound, one-slot-full, ISR/main-loop interleavings, and the watchdog-sized drain, but keep the refactor scope to `TelemetryBuffer`, `FlashJournal`, and their tests. If the correct answer is to reject part of the proposed split because of ISR timing, say so explicitly. I am happy to trade cleverness for something I can understand next Saturday.

