I need a careful review of an intermittent UI freeze on an STM32H7 device with a 480x272 RGB565 display. After 20-40 minutes of touch activity, the screen stops updating while the control loop continues to run. A watchdog reset is not triggered. The issue is most common when a modal is opened during a DMA2D blit. This is production firmware, so please reason about ownership, interrupt context, cache maintenance, and lifetime before suggesting a patch. Do not assume the display controller and the UI thread share a coherent cache.

Relevant paths:
`firmware/ui/ScreenManager.cpp`
`firmware/ui/FrameBufferPool.cpp`
`drivers/display/LtdcDma.cpp`
`platform/CacheOps.cpp`
`tests/ui/test_framebuffer.cpp`

Simplified code:
```cpp
void ScreenManager::present() {
    auto frame = pool_.acquire();
    render(frame->pixels(), model_);
    dma_.start(frame->address(), [this, frame] {
        pool_.release(frame);
        presented_.notify();
    });
}

Frame* FrameBufferPool::acquire() {
    std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mu_);
    return free_.empty() ? front_ : free_.pop_back();
}
```
`dma_.start` invokes its callback from `HAL_DMA2D_TransferCompleteCallback`, which runs on the DMA interrupt path. `Frame` is reference-counted in the desktop build, but the target build uses an intrusive counter with `release()` implemented in `FrameBuffer.cpp`.

Observed log:
```text
[1842.117] UI present frame=17 addr=0x2406C000 state=FREE
[1842.118] DMA2D start src=0x2406C000 len=261120
[1842.119] TOUCH modal=open
[1842.119] UI present frame=17 addr=0x2406C000 state=IN_FLIGHT
[1842.120] DMA2D complete status=OK
[1842.120] cache clean range=0x2406C000..0x240AC000
[1842.120] assert skipped: ISR context
[1842.121] UI heartbeat=1
```
Sometimes the last line is absent; sometimes the heartbeat continues but the panel remains unchanged. A logic-analyzer trace shows the LTDC layer address changes only once during the failure window.

Please trace the possible state transitions and identify the minimal root cause or roots, including any race that the desktop tests would miss. Then propose a reviewable fix with explicit ISR/thread boundaries, correct cache clean/invalidate timing, and a test strategy that can run on host plus target hardware. Call out any assumptions about `free_`, `front_`, callback lifetime, and whether `present()` may be re-entered. I need a prioritized diagnosis and patch plan, not a broad rewrite.

